<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afonso I of Kongo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1509">1509</a>–<a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Afonso I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afonso I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, also known as Nzinga Mbemba, was king of the Kongo Empire from 1509 to 1543. A committed Catholic, he promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, literacy and ties with Portugal, while trying to strengthen royal authority. His surviving letters to the Portuguese crown protest the damage caused by the growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo.jpg" alt="Afonso I of Kongo" width="293" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Affricoll9 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manikongo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
